  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so there is zero evidence about whether multimodal fusion adds value over a single modality, which encoder is strongest, or which design choices matter. The most fundamental uncertainty is the performance ceiling of each individual modality. 2) What this tests: config_004 (image_only, singleprofile, imagenet_vit_l) establishes a strong single-modality baseline using the most powerful and well-validated image encoder in the available set (ViT-L pretrained on ImageNet). It is simple—no aggregation or fusion—so any performance is attributable purely to the image representation. 3) Why it is the best first experiment: Under a limited budget, a clean, strong baseline is the most informative anchor. Every subsequent multimodal experiment can be interpreted relative to this number: if multimodal configurations fail to exceed it, the added complexity is not justified; if they do, the gain is quantified. Starting with the strongest available single-modality model also avoids the risk of underestimating the baseline (which would make multimodal gains look artificially large). This single experiment reduces the largest source of uncertainty before any fusion, aggregation, or profile-setting decisions are made."
